Sure — here's a small exercise that simulates a real PR workflow. Try the questions yourself before reading the solution. 🧪 Exercise: Inspect Staged Changes Before a Pull Request Scenario You're working on a student portal. You've created a branch called fix-login to fix a login validation bug. You made two changes: login.js → your actual bug fix, which you staged. README.md → an unrelated documentation change, which you did not stage. Your job is to determine exactly what would go into your next commit and what your PR contains. Part 1 — Create the repository Run: Bashmkdir pr-inspection-exercise cd pr-inspection-exercise git init Create the initial application: Bashcat > login.js <<'EOF' function login(username, password) { return authenticate(username, password); } EOF Create a README: Bashcat > README.md <<'EOF' # Student Portal A simple student portal application. EOF Commit both: Bashgit add . git commit -m "Initial student portal" Now create your feature branch: Bashgit switch -c fix-login Your graph is: A ─── B ↑ fix-login Part 2 — Make your actual fix Modify login.js: Bashpython3 - <<'PY' from pathlib import Path p = Path("login.js") s = p.read_text() s = s.replace( 'function login(username, password) {
', 'function login(username, password) {
' ' if (!username || !password) {
' ' return "Missing credentials";
' ' }
' ) p.write_text(s) PY Stage it: Bashgit add login.js Part 3 — Make an unrelated change Now modify the README: Bashcat >> README.md <<'EOF' ## Development Run the application locally with Node.js. EOF Don't stage this change. Your state is now: HEAD │ ▼ A ─── B │ ├── STAGED │ └── login.js │ └── UNSTAGED └── README.md 📝 Your task Before looking at the solution, answer these: 1. What command checks the overall repository state? 2. What command shows which files are staged? 3. What command shows the exact staged code changes? 4. What command shows your README change that isn't staged? 5. If you commit right now, will the README change be included? 6. What should you check before opening the PR? ✅ Solution 1. Check the overall state Run: Bashgit status You should see something similar to: On branch fix-login Changes to be committed: modified: login.js Changes not staged for commit: modified: README.md This immediately tells you that the two changes are separated. 2. Check staged files Run: Bashgit diff --cached --name-only Expected: login.js So only login.js is currently staged. 3. Inspect the actual staged change Run: Bashgit diff --cached Expected: Diff function login(username, password) { + if (!username || !password) { + return "Missing credentials"; + } return authenticate(username, password); } This is the important inspection. You're checking: "Is this exactly the change I want to put into my commit?" 4. Inspect unstaged changes Run: Bashgit diff Expected: Diff # Student Portal A simple student portal application. + +## Development + +Run the application locally with Node.js. This confirms that the README change is not staged. 5. Would the README be committed? No. If you run: Bashgit commit -m "Fix login validation" Git commits the staged login.js change. The README modification remains unstaged. After committing, you'd have: A ─── B ─── C ↑ fix-login with: C = login.js fix while the README modification remains in your working tree. 6. Check the branch before opening the PR First update your knowledge of the remote: Bashgit fetch origin Then inspect the commits that your branch has beyond main: Bashgit log --oneline origin/main..HEAD And inspect the branch's actual PR changes: Bashgit diff origin/main...HEAD This is different from: Bashgit diff --cached because after you commit, the login fix is no longer staged — it's part of your branch's history. Final check Before opening the PR: Bashgit status git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD git diff origin/main...HEAD You want to see that: PR: login.js ↓ login validation fix Not included: unrelated README change If you intentionally want the README change to stay local, leave it unstaged. If it should be part of the PR, review it separately and stage/commit it intentionally. The three commands to remember Bashgit diff --cached → What am I about to commit? Bashgit diff → What else have I changed locally? Bashgit diff origin/main...HEAD → What does my branch contain for the PR? Golden rule: inspect first, commit second, open the PR only after you've verified the complete branch difference.
